      "calculationScope": "EXACT_MANUAL_CALCULATION",
      "manualArithmetic": "No assigned numerical exercise. Reviewer probes: N=80N and mu_s=2/5 give a 32N threshold; a 20N static pull needs -20N friction; 40N exceeds the threshold. If sliding, mu_d=3/10 gives 24N friction and (40-24)/8=2m/s². k=250N/m and x=±1/25m give F=∓10N. Independently integrate the spherical-shell angular kernel: inside, potential -GM/a is constant; outside, -GM/r. Uniform density gives M(r)=4pi*rho*r³/3 and g=4pi*G*rho*r/3, whose center limit is zero. No numerical G or pi evaluation is requested by the source.",
      "manualFeasibility": "PASS_AI_EXACT",
      "physicalAssumptions": "Nonnegative dry-friction coefficients. Static friction adapts in the stated initially resting, slow-pull situation and is bounded, rather than universally mu_s*N. Kinetic friction opposes relative sliding; mu_d*N is the explicitly approximate dry-contact model. Hooke law applies in the linear elastic regime, with signed deformation from natural length. Gravity applies to point masses or nonoverlapping spherically symmetric masses, with center distance R. The interior shell result requires spherical symmetry; linear internal g additionally requires uniform density. Local mg assumes approximately uniform g over a small height.",
      "pedagogicalReview": "Threshold comparison precedes accepting rest, and static inequality is distinguished from the kinetic model. Signed Hooke restoration explains the minus sign. Separates external inverse-square gravity, interior enclosed mass and local uniform g; it does not extrapolate GM/r² through the center. Reviewer exact examples are explicitly labelled as constructed probes.",
      "assetChecksums": {
